====================================================================================================
HOST-RESIDENT PROCESS COST PER DEVICE  (architecture 2, no-Flink scaling experiment)
====================================================================================================
This file documents the cost of host-side processes that run OUTSIDE Docker for every device added
to the pipeline.  cAdvisor / Prometheus / Grafana only see container-level metrics, so these
processes are absent from the resources_a2_scaling.{csv,log} report.  They are however the
dominant memory cost of the experiment and explain the saturation curve observed at high device
counts.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Per-device, steady-state (mature, warmed-up JVMs / Python coordinator)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Process                                              | RSS   | Role
  ---------------------------------------------------- | ------| --------------------------------------
  mvn exec:java MqttProducerBatches                    | ~460 MiB | Reads CSV, publishes 1 msg/sec to Mosquitto (:1884)
  mvn exec:java MqttConsumerBatches                    | ~460 MiB | Bridges Mosquitto -> Pulsar (${device} bytes topic)
  mvn exec:java MqttHelperClientBatches                | ~460 MiB | Pulsar bytes -> Avro Sensor -> ${device}_model_consume
  python3 pulsarConsumer60Batches.py                   | ~ 92 MiB | Subscribes Avro, batches 60 msgs, writes Postgres,
                                                        |          spawns Forecasting.py subprocess every ~60 s
  ---------------------------------------------------- | ------| --------------------------------------
  TOTAL STEADY-STATE per device                        | ~1.47 GiB

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Decomposition of each ~460 MiB mvn JVM
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Component                                       | Approx
  ----------------------------------------------- | -------
  Maven launcher (Plexus classloader chain)       |  80-100 MiB
  exec:java plugin + dependency resolution        |  30- 50 MiB
  JVM base (committed heap, stacks, GC, code cache, native)
                                                  | 100-150 MiB
  Metaspace (class metadata)                      |  70-100 MiB
  Application heap (Pulsar / Jackson / Avro / MQTT clients,
                    in-flight messages, buffers)  |  80-130 MiB
  ----------------------------------------------- | -------
  TOTAL                                           | 360-530 MiB

  Note: roughly ~70 % of each JVM's RSS is Maven + JVM tax, not application logic.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Decomposition of the 92 MiB Python coordinator
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Component                                       | Approx
  ----------------------------------------------- | -------
  CPython interpreter base                        |  10 MiB
  pulsar-client (with C++ binding)                |  30 MiB
  pandas + numpy (loaded on first DataFrame)      |  30 MiB
  psycopg2 + joblib + standard library imports    |  15 MiB
  Working memory (60-row DataFrame, accumulators) |   5 MiB
  ----------------------------------------------- | -------
  TOTAL                                           |  90 MiB

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Transient cost: Forecasting.py subprocess
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Every ~60 seconds, the Python coordinator spawns:
    python3 Forecasting.py --device_name deviceN --bucket_name missingtimestamp

The subprocess imports TensorFlow + Keras (~500-800 MiB transient), runs LSTM inference on 60
samples, writes 60 forecasted rows to Postgres, then exits and frees its memory.

  - Single-device:   one TensorFlow process at a time, ~10 s lifetime.
  - 20-device case:  if the per-device flush schedule aligns near minute boundaries,
                     5-15 simultaneous TensorFlow subprocesses may briefly coexist,
                     adding 3-12 GiB of transient memory pressure on top of the steady-state.

This is the spikiest part of host-memory usage during the scaling run.  Because it is
transient and aperiodic, it is not directly visible in 15-s Grafana sampling — but it
correlates strongly with the "low-coverage" tail devices in the delay report (every late-phase
device's first forecast triggers right when system pressure is highest).

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Total host-process load vs concurrent device count
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  #devices  | host-process RSS    (rough)    | running on a 30 GiB host
  --------- | ------------------------------ | ------------------------
       1    |   1.5 GiB                      |   5 % of RAM
       5    |   7.4 GiB                      |  25 %
      10    |  14.7 GiB                      |  49 %
      15    |  22.1 GiB                      |  74 %
      19    |  27.9 GiB                      |  93 %
      20    |  29.4 GiB  ( + transient ~5 GiB peak from forecasting subprocesses )
                                                 -> swap territory, OOM risk

This is the structural reason both architectures (1 = with Flink, 2 = no Flink) saturate near
20 concurrent devices on a 30 GiB host: the deployment style (one full Maven-launched JVM per
producer/bridge/sink and a per-device Python coordinator) dominates host memory, regardless of
the streaming runtime.  The Flink containers in architecture 1 add ~2 GiB on top, which is why
that architecture saturates slightly earlier than architecture 2 in practice.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
What to do differently next time (sized for impact)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Change                                                                | Per-JVM saving | At 20 devices
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------|--------------
  MAVEN_OPTS="-Xmx128m -XX:MaxMetaspaceSize=96m"                        | ~150-200 MiB   | ~ 9-12 GiB
  Replace mvn exec:java with `java -cp target/*.jar <main-class>`       | ~100-150 MiB   | ~ 6- 9 GiB
  Merge MqttProducerBatches + MqttConsumerBatches + MqttHelperClientBatches into one JVM
                                                                        | (3 -> 1 JVMs)  | ~12-15 GiB

  Any one of the three buys enough headroom for 20 devices to fit comfortably in 30 GiB
  with no swap. The middle option is the smallest code change (build one fat JAR, update
  start_app_no_flink.sh's invocation) and saves both Maven and the launcher overhead.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Why this isn't in the Grafana CSV exports
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
cAdvisor (the scraper feeding Prometheus, which feeds Grafana) only observes the cgroups of
Docker-managed containers.  Host-resident processes — anything started by `mvn exec:java` or
`python3` from the launcher script — live in the host's own cgroup hierarchy outside Docker's
view.  Without process-exporter (per-process Linux /proc scraping) or node-exporter, these
processes contribute zero data points to resources_a2_scaling.{csv,log}.

To capture them in a future run, add to docker-compose.yml:
    process-exporter:
      image: ncabatoff/process-exporter
      command: --procnames "java,python3"  --procfs /host/proc
      volumes:
        - /proc:/host/proc:ro
      pid: host
    Then add a scrape_config in prometheus.yml.

Until that's in place, this log file is the (manual) record of host-process cost for the
architecture-2 scaling experiment.
====================================================================================================
